    "summary": "The United Nations has officially ended its political mission in Somalia, known as UNSOM, marking the closure of a 12-year operation that supported peacebuilding and state-building efforts.",
    "content": "The United Nations has formally concluded its political mission in Somalia, ending a presence that began in 2013. The mission, known as the United Nations Assistance Mission in Somalia (UNSOM), was established to support the federal government's peace and reconciliation efforts and to advise on governance, security, and development.\n\nUNSOM's mandate covered a wide range of activities, including good offices, political advice, and coordination of international support. It played a role in facilitating dialogue among Somali stakeholders and assisting in the implementation of national strategies. The mission also worked closely with the African Union and other partners.\n\nThe closure comes amid a transition in international support for Somalia, with responsibilities increasingly being handed over to the Somali government and other UN agencies. The drawdown of UNSOM follows a request from the Somali authorities for a phased transfer of tasks. The UN Security Council had previously extended the mission's mandate while planning for its eventual exit.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the end of UNSOM's political mission signals a shift in how international engagement in Somalia will be structured. Many diaspora members have family ties to Somalia and have invested in its stability and development. The closure may affect channels for political dialogue and coordination that the diaspora has sometimes relied on to engage with the federal government and international community.\n\nAs the UN transitions its presence, the focus will be on ensuring that gains in state-building and reconciliation are sustained. The Somali government will need to assume greater responsibility for political mediation and coordination with international partners. The diaspora's role in supporting these efforts remains significant, as remittances and investments continue to be vital for the country's economy.\n\nThe conclusion of UNSOM's mandate does not mean the end of UN engagement in Somalia. Other UN agencies and programs will continue to operate, addressing humanitarian and development needs. The political mission's exit is part of a broader reconfiguration of international support as Somalia continues its path toward stability.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/qaramada-midoobay-oo-si-rasmi-ah-u-soo-afjartay-howlgalkeedii-siyaasadeed-ee-soomaaliya/427067/",
